WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.596
Hi, good morning, Ziad.

00:00:01.596 --> 00:00:02.984
Hope you slept okay.

00:00:02.984 --> 00:00:07.635
It's Wednesday, November twenty-fifth, the day before Thanksgiving.

00:00:07.635 --> 00:00:12.007
A cold, clear one, with a normal workday and a wide open night.

00:00:12.007 --> 00:00:14.853
Here's one to think about while you move.

00:00:14.853 --> 00:00:21.099
If you double the distance between shelf brackets, about how much more does the shelf sag?

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:22.487
Answer's at the end.

00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:26.652
Weather's easy today, sunny and dry, staying in the forties.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:27.970
Tomorrow's the one.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:39.492
Rain starts around late morning, gets heavy through the afternoon and evening, one to two inches, with gusts maybe up near fifty, so Thursday's not a bike day at all.

00:00:39.492 --> 00:00:40.463
Okay, the day.

00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:43.586
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets.

00:00:43.586 --> 00:00:45.530
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.542
After that, nothing on there.

00:00:47.542 --> 00:00:54.136
So if there's anything you need to pick up before the holiday, tonight's the dry window for it.

00:00:54.136 --> 00:00:55.108
Alright, news.

00:00:55.108 --> 00:00:58.231
The chip selloff kind of came back overnight.

00:00:58.231 --> 00:01:02.395
Monday had that record bounce when Nvidia brought its C.E.O.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.588
back, but by this morning in Asia it was gone.

00:01:05.588 --> 00:01:18.289
Taiwan's market fell almost eight percent at one point, South Korea's more than six, and the companies that make chips and chip machines dropped something like ten to fifteen percent.

00:01:18.289 --> 00:01:20.857
The worry underneath is the same one.

00:01:20.857 --> 00:01:23.564
People are just less sure all this A.I.

00:01:23.564 --> 00:01:24.813
spending pays off.

00:01:24.813 --> 00:01:27.034
Oh, actually, here's a good one.

00:01:27.034 --> 00:01:34.391
A small study took about six hundred heavy ChatGPT users and had some of them stop using it for two weeks.

00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:41.678
The people who took the break said they felt less lonely, and they spent more time with people in person.

00:01:41.678 --> 00:01:44.524
It's one small study, so hold it loosely.

00:01:44.524 --> 00:01:45.843
And closer to home.

00:01:45.843 --> 00:01:55.212
A federal appeals court gave Trump a new opening to move his Manhattan hush money conviction out of state court and into federal court.

00:01:55.212 --> 00:02:02.292
The judges said the lower court got it wrong when it said no, and sent it back for a quick new ruling.

00:02:02.292 --> 00:02:07.358
If it moves, his lawyers get another shot at throwing the conviction out.

00:02:07.358 --> 00:02:10.898
Okay, here's one for a slow afternoon this weekend.

00:02:10.898 --> 00:02:19.018
Book Club Bar, the bookstore that's also a bar, has a Bushwick spot on Troutman Street, right off the Jefferson stop.

00:02:19.018 --> 00:02:22.350
It opened this spring, so it's still pretty new.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:29.984
Apparently the poetry section is way bigger than at their original store, and there's a small press shelf too.

00:02:29.984 --> 00:02:32.552
Grab an Irish coffee and just browse.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.912
Today's challenge is a making one.

00:02:34.912 --> 00:02:39.146
Tonight, make one thing by hand that you'd normally just buy.

00:02:39.146 --> 00:02:46.295
A jar of salad dressing, a pot of soup, a batch of granola, whatever's easy with what's in the kitchen.

00:02:46.295 --> 00:02:49.973
Twenty minutes, no recipe hunting if you can help it.

00:02:49.973 --> 00:02:55.040
Then tomorrow, stuck inside in the rain, it's just there waiting for you.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.608
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:02:57.608 --> 00:03:03.785
Remember noticing that to-dos left out in plain sight don't actually get done any faster?

00:03:03.785 --> 00:03:05.450
There's a name for that.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:06.283
Habituation.

00:03:06.283 --> 00:03:10.448
Habituation is about the simplest kind of learning there is.

00:03:10.448 --> 00:03:16.972
When something keeps showing up and nothing happens, your brain slowly stops responding to it.

00:03:16.972 --> 00:03:22.038
The ticking clock, the hum of the fridge, the sticky note on the monitor.

00:03:22.038 --> 00:03:24.467
It isn't that you stop seeing them.

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:28.771
Your brain has just filed them under safe, nothing to do here.

00:03:28.771 --> 00:03:31.477
The classic proof came from a sea slug.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:34.254
Poke it gently and it pulls its gill in.

00:03:34.254 --> 00:03:38.418
Keep poking and nothing bad happens, and it stops bothering.

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:45.705
Researchers traced that to the actual connections between its nerve cells getting weaker with repetition.

00:03:45.705 --> 00:03:48.551
That work ended up winning a Nobel Prize.

00:03:48.551 --> 00:03:50.911
So it's not laziness, it's wiring.

00:03:50.911 --> 00:03:53.201
And a few details make it useful.

00:03:53.201 --> 00:03:54.173
It's specific.

00:03:54.173 --> 00:04:01.599
You tune out that exact note in that exact spot, but change it, even a little, and it's back on your radar.

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:03.612
That's called dishabituation.

00:04:03.612 --> 00:04:05.902
It also wears off with time away.

00:04:05.902 --> 00:04:09.234
Leave for a week and the clock ticks loud again.

00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:13.120
And it's different from your eyes or ears getting tired.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.035
A new sound still gets through right away.

00:04:16.035 --> 00:04:17.979
So here's the practical bit.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:22.074
A reminder that never changes is going to fade, guaranteed.

00:04:22.074 --> 00:04:27.973
It works better if you move it, rewrite it, or tie it to a moment instead of a place.

00:04:27.973 --> 00:04:32.484
Something like, when I sit down with coffee, I do that one thing.

00:04:32.484 --> 00:04:37.829
A moment you hit once a day stays new in a way a note on the wall just can't.

00:04:37.829 --> 00:04:42.618
So: calendar says gym at seven, Godinger till five, and a free night.

00:04:42.618 --> 00:04:44.214
Make one thing by hand.

00:04:44.214 --> 00:04:50.460
The answer: about sixteen times as much, since sag goes with the span to the fourth power.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.000
Have a good Wednesday, Ziad, and stay dry tomorrow.
